# =============================================================================
# SECRETS — never commit anything matching these
# =============================================================================
# See documentation.md, "Secrets and what must never be committed".
#
# tokens.py holds the Square API credentials. It is ignored in
# pi4-software/.gitignore as well; the duplication is deliberate, because a
# credential file is the one thing where a belt-and-braces rule costs nothing.
tokens.py
secrets.py
credentials.json
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*

# Python bytecode.
#
# THIS IS A SECRETS RULE, NOT A TIDINESS RULE. A .pyc embeds the string
# constants of the module it was compiled from, so importing an ignored
# tokens.py produces __pycache__/tokens.cpython-*.pyc containing the access
# token in plain text — and that file is NOT covered by the tokens.py rule
# above. Exactly that happened here and reached a public GitHub repo; see
# documentation.md for the incident and the rotation that followed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# =============================================================================
# Working documents — deliberately NOT part of the repository
# =============================================================================
# Planning and agent-instruction files. These are working documents between the
# author and Claude Code, not deliverables, and they were never committed.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, so it is not a surprise later: a `git pull` on the Pi or a fresh
# clone by a collaborator will NOT contain these. Anything another machine needs
# to act on belongs in documentation.md or a README, both of which are tracked.
CLAUDE.md
claude_code_integration_prompt.md
dashboard.md
dashboard-plan.md
plan.md

# =============================================================================
# Build output
# =============================================================================
# The names README.md's install steps use, plus anything else matching, since a
# second build tree (a different Pi link backend, say) is a normal thing to want
# and a build directory is never worth committing.
build/
build-*/
build-baseline.txt

# =============================================================================
# Generated data — the fridge database, camera frames, Grafana's own state
# =============================================================================
# The database is machine-local operational data, not source. The -wal and -shm
# files are SQLite's write-ahead log and shared-memory index: they are part of
# the database, so committing one without the others would produce a file that
# opens but is missing the most recent writes.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Camera frames written by picapture for the dashboard to display.
pi4-software/frames/

# Grafana's own database and logs when it is run from inside the repo during
# development. The dashboards themselves ARE committed, as exported JSON in
# pi4-software/grafana/dashboards/ — see dashboard-plan.md stage D2 for why:
# a dashboard that exists only inside Grafana is lost with Grafana.
grafana-data/

# =============================================================================
# Machine-local editor and toolchain settings
# =============================================================================
# These carry absolute paths from whoever generated them, so they are personal
# to one machine rather than to the project.
#
# NOT ignored, deliberately: rp2040-software/.vscode/cmake-kits.json. It refers
# to the toolchain through ${env:USERPROFILE} rather than a hardcoded path, so
# it is portable, and it is how the team gets the right ARM kit selected.
.vscode/settings.json
compile_commands.json

# =============================================================================
# Third-party documents we do not have redistribution rights to
# =============================================================================
datasheets/
